#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083056 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083244 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "145eff6a-ad61-5001-ab14-cf77e93a7958" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Tangent Line to a Parametric Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "879e41c3-7d67-5821-81dc-f6d3fc664ffd" :question/math-academy-id "e-597" :question/problem "Suppose that we have a curve defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=2t+3, \\quad y=t^3-4t, \\qquad -\\infty < t <\\infty.\n$$\n\nWhat is the equation of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $t=-1?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point by substituting $t=-1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2(-1)+3=1, \\\\ y & =(-1)^{3}-4(-1)=-1+4=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the tangent line to the curve at the point $(1,3).$ The plot below shows the curve and the tangent line at this point.\n\n![](images/0f/0fa3fc12e83e2a581499680145df9229917c7426e6a3d12c51d88e115a42033e.png)\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we need to find $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}.$ To compute it, we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx'(t) = 2, \\qquad y'(t) = 3t^2-4 .\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{y'(t)}{x'(t)} = \\dfrac{3t^2-4}{2}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=-1,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=-1} & =\\frac{3(-1)^{2}-4}{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the equation of the tangent line, we use point-slope form with the point $(x_1,y_1) = (1,3)$ and the slope $m=-\\dfrac{1}{2}.$ We get\n\n$$\ny - 3 = -\\frac{1}{2}\\left(x-1\\right).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083057 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083245 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "898e04b8-1b7e-59e7-b03f-3ece54aa093b" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Normal Line to a Parametric Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "db8c5a47-27b8-548d-8bd7-65289cff06da" :question/math-academy-id "e-598" :question/problem "![](images/9d/9dcc4c97bd296ef4627b02b6cfafcdb643f475cd8dacd36a820db760bd6fb045.png)\n\nThe following parametric equations define the curve shown above:\n\n$$\nx=2\\cos{t} +\\sin{2t},\\quad y=\\cos{t} - 2\\sin{2t},\\qquad 0\\leq t<2\\pi\n$$\n\nCalculate the equation of the normal to the curve at the point where $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ (shown as a dashed line in the plot above)." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point by substituting $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}+1, \\\\  \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-2(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the normal at the point $\\left(\\sqrt{2}+1,\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} -2\\right).$ For this, we need to find $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx'(t) = -2\\sin t + 2\\cos 2t, \\qquad y'(t) = -\\sin t - 4 \\cos 2t .\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4},$ the slope $m$ of the tangent line is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=\\pi /4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}))} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{2}+4(0)}{2(0-\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope $m_n$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m$:\n\n$$\nm_n = -\\dfrac{1}{m} = -\\dfrac{1}{\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)} = -2\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the equation of the normal line, we use the point-slope form \n\n$$\ny - y_1 = m_n(x-x_1),\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n(x_1,y_1) = \\left(\\sqrt{2}+1,\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} -2\\right), \\qquad m_n=-2,\n$$\n\nand we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-2) & =-2(x-(\\sqrt{2}+1)) \\\\ y-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+2 & =-2x+2\\sqrt{2}+2 \\\\ y+2x & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083074 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083278 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fc81cba-b997-54bc-9e11-0a4ff130833e" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Points Where the Tangent is Horizontal or Vertical"}] :question/id #uuid "72076ddb-4a3a-530f-854c-7b1400cd9859" :question/math-academy-id "e-6240" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=t^2+3, \\quad y=-t+1, \\qquad -\\infty <     t <    \\infty.\n$$\n\nDetermine the value of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is vertical." :question/worked-solution "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm = \\dfrac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is vertical if $x'(t)=0$ while $y'(t) \\neq 0 .$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nx'(t) = 2t, \\qquad y'(t) = -1 .\n$$\n\nSolving $x'(t) = 0$ gives\n\n$$\n2t=0\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad t=0.\n$$\n\nNote that $y'(0) = -1\\neq 0.$ Therefore, we conclude that when $t = 0,$ the tangent to the curve is vertical."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083075 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083280 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "12a5ad95-0cc9-53c5-8e1c-dae35d1c4447" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Points Where the Normal is Horizontal or Vertical"}] :question/id #uuid "b2f8d797-75e8-5ac8-82e7-1c5a9788e6e3" :question/math-academy-id "e-6252" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=2t^3-1,\\quad y=t^2-t+1,\\qquad-\\infty < t <\\infty.\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the **normal** to the curve is horizontal." :question/worked-solution "The normal is horizontal if the tangent is vertical. Therefore, we require $x'(t)=0$ while $y'(t) \\neq 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=6{t}^{2},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=2t-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving $x'(t) = 0$ gives\n\n$$\n6t^2 = 0 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad t = 0.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(0) & =2\\cdot 0-1=-1\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed horizontal when $t = 0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527919 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083278 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fc81cba-b997-54bc-9e11-0a4ff130833e" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Points Where the Tangent is Horizontal or Vertical"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28911cb9-7322-5a1f-986f-3dbf31e52f20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527399} {:answer/id #uuid "452bd0eb-ccef-5923-ae11-2e1791e47de6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527442} {:answer/id #uuid "45bc4874-5ffb-59e4-b289-5becfcd19d2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527443} {:answer/id #uuid "af69f25d-e970-508f-975c-92885b986a3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527667} {:answer/id #uuid "cc6452f1-2d28-5302-beb6-29f15c7c0511" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527719}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "45bc4874-5ffb-59e4-b289-5becfcd19d2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527443} :answer-field/id #uuid "4edb52f5-c266-5ff9-af4d-1f03393c883c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527836}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "14b24104-4820-5739-b269-6386c5a0fdfc" :question/math-academy-id "q-122472" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=3{t}^{4},\\,y=8t,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nDetermine the value of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is vertical." :question/worked-solution "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is vertical if ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${y}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=12{t}^{3},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=8.\n$$\n\nSolving ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n12{t}^{3}=0\\,⟹\\,t=0.\n$$\n\nNote that ${y}^{′}(0)=8\\ne 0.$ Therefore, we conclude that when $t=0$ the tangent to the curve is vertical."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527943 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083244 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "145eff6a-ad61-5001-ab14-cf77e93a7958" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Tangent Line to a Parametric Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c9eaf20-c666-5150-bbf7-5cdd25f3858a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-5=6(x-\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527371} {:answer/id #uuid "23a36e5a-2d17-540d-a480-934ae5ff5398" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-5=\\sqrt{2}(x-\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527387} {:answer/id #uuid "51715b4c-e8ae-5dee-8559-fabf91340f14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+5=2\\sqrt{3}(x+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527462} {:answer/id #uuid "b8e00b9e-7f09-5e90-bc56-bbe95b5482c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-5=6\\sqrt{2}(x-\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527689} {:answer/id #uuid "fdfe79b9-6624-5d5d-a154-82e31819e4aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+\\sqrt{2}=\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}(x+5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527798}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b8e00b9e-7f09-5e90-bc56-bbe95b5482c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-5=6\\sqrt{2}(x-\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527689} :answer-field/id #uuid "856fdf82-8f4f-508d-812e-b4bde879e8dc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527864}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5579ca0e-f803-5d9f-87c0-8c8636fd4e81" :question/math-academy-id "q-122584" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{t+1},\\,y={t}^{3}+4,\\,-1<t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nCalculate the equation of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point by substituting $t=1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\sqrt{1+1}=\\sqrt{2}, \\\\ y & =(1)^{3}+4=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the tangent at the point $(\\sqrt{2},5).$ \n\nTo find the equation of the tangent line, we need to find $\\frac{dy}{dx}.$ To compute it, we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t+1}},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=3{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{t}^{2}}{(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t+1}})} \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}\\sqrt{(t+1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=1,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=1} & =6\\cdot {1}^{2}\\sqrt{1+1} \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the equation of the tangent line, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(\\sqrt{2},5)$ and the slope $m=6\\sqrt{2}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-5 & =6\\sqrt{2}(x-\\sqrt{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527989 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083244 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "145eff6a-ad61-5001-ab14-cf77e93a7958" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Tangent Line to a Parametric Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "890682ad-7bd6-5e47-a193-c49b79f9309a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\,\\frac{16}{7})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527576} {:answer/id #uuid "8aa24a59-28c0-5ddf-af4a-9ea620581550" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\,\\frac{16}{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527581} {:answer/id #uuid "8f1ded1a-3543-58c8-aa2a-3a66aeaf334f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\,-\\frac{5}{7})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527589} {:answer/id #uuid "c456bbd6-ff27-5bbd-b987-50a276c4e8b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\,\\frac{12}{7})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527699} {:answer/id #uuid "e77c3742-a6e4-525c-89a7-67b1faaea9f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\,-\\frac{2}{7})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527763}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "890682ad-7bd6-5e47-a193-c49b79f9309a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\,\\frac{16}{7})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527576} :answer-field/id #uuid "45604a60-69c4-5549-aadb-af6a3764d454" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527831}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c3009bb8-76e4-5e7a-be7f-b556ddcfdaef" :question/math-academy-id "q-122700" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={t}^{2}+3t,\\,y=1+\\sqrt{2t},\\,t\\in [0,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=2$ on the curve. Determine the point at which the tangent to the curve at $C$ crosses the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=2$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =(2)^{2}+3(2)=10, \\\\ y & =1+\\sqrt{2(2)}=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the tangent line to the curve at the point $(10,3).$ \n\nTo find the equation of the tangent line, we need to find $\\frac{dy}{dx}.$ To compute it, we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=2t+3,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2t}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2t}}}{2t+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2t}(2t+3)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=2,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=2} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\cdot 2}(2\\cdot 2+3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{14}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the equation of the tangent line, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(10,3)$ and the slope $m=\\frac{1}{14}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-3 & =\\frac{1}{14}(x-10).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe tangent crosses the $y$-axis when $x=0.$ At this point, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-3 & =\\frac{1}{14}(0-10) \\\\ y-3 & =-\\frac{5}{7} \\\\ y & =\\frac{16}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $(0,\\,\\frac{16}{7})$ is the $y$-intercept."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527946 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083280 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "12a5ad95-0cc9-53c5-8e1c-dae35d1c4447" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Points Where the Normal is Horizontal or Vertical"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7e20b7b8-1e36-5f41-9b20-23e8c8a99d75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527548} {:answer/id #uuid "a5f3cdeb-e33c-5ce4-8657-a90ffab15d8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527648} {:answer/id #uuid "c86857df-a7de-55aa-bacf-e267b3b6b6f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527710} {:answer/id #uuid "cc85ea11-173a-5a41-bdb2-37322253a23d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527720} {:answer/id #uuid "e7a66421-49c0-5cde-af39-90c0d6317866" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527764}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cc85ea11-173a-5a41-bdb2-37322253a23d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527720} :answer-field/id #uuid "245554b4-93be-561f-a213-763e72867446" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527813}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5b067330-3793-583e-b08f-f7fd948895ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-122836" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={t}^{4}+2{t}^{2},\\,y={t}^{3}-3t,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nDetermine the value of the parameter $t$ at which the normal line to the curve is horizontal." :question/worked-solution "The normal line is horizontal when the tangent line to the curve is vertical. Therefore, we require ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${y}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=4{t}^{3}+4t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=3{t}^{2}-3\n$$\n\nSolving ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n4t({t}^{2}+1)=0\\,⟹\\,t=0.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(0)=3(0)^{2}-3=-3\\ne 0,\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed horizontal when $t=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528013 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083245 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "898e04b8-1b7e-59e7-b03f-3ece54aa093b" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Normal Line to a Parametric Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "081ac015-24cb-5663-8688-ae1b7ff7348b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{4}{\\,e\\,}x+\\frac{2{e}^{2}-4}{\\,e\\,}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527322} {:answer/id #uuid "434c5620-db8a-5f85-858a-d1decc931c4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2}{\\,e\\,}x+\\frac{4{e}^{2}-2}{\\,e\\,}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527437} {:answer/id #uuid "a8cc7b58-1e78-5215-ae55-4b924f699a54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2}{\\,e\\,}x-\\frac{2{e}^{2}-4}{\\,e\\,}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527653} {:answer/id #uuid "b63fdc98-6ffe-5091-898f-611ee1b4b7de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{\\,e\\,}x+\\frac{2{e}^{2}+4}{\\,e\\,}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527679} {:answer/id #uuid "cd19d4c1-53f7-5df5-ae10-f2400625057c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{\\,e\\,}x+\\frac{2{e}^{2}-4}{\\,e\\,}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527723}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b63fdc98-6ffe-5091-898f-611ee1b4b7de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{\\,e\\,}x+\\frac{2{e}^{2}+4}{\\,e\\,}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527679} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac112813-4b74-55f0-8f43-22f46db91806" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527881}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f508d724-e816-5421-91b6-b8baa491ba13" :question/math-academy-id "q-122928" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,\\,y=2t\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,\\,0<t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=e$ on the curve. Find the equation of the normal to the curve at $C.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=e$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡e=4, \\\\ y & =2e\\operatorname{ln}⁡e=2e\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the tangent line to the curve at the point $(4,2e).$ For this, we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\frac{4}{t},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+2\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+2}{(\\frac{4}{t})} \\\\  & =\\frac{t\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+t}{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=e,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=e} & =\\frac{e\\operatorname{ln}⁡e+e}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{e+e}{2}=e\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope ${m}_{n}$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m$:\n\n$$\n{m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{\\,e\\,}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the equation of the normal, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(4,2e)$ and the slope ${m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{\\,e\\,}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-2e & =(-\\frac{1}{\\,e\\,})(x-4) \\\\ y-2e & =-\\frac{1}{\\,e\\,}x+\\frac{4}{\\,e\\,} \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{\\,e\\,}x+\\frac{2{e}^{2}+4}{\\,e\\,}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527922 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083245 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "898e04b8-1b7e-59e7-b03f-3ece54aa093b" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Normal Line to a Parametric Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "041f8bb1-395a-5761-a87c-1b044587e237" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527315} {:answer/id #uuid "5577dcb2-91aa-5255-8e1f-f21bfb8168c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527470} {:answer/id #uuid "62850344-0899-50d9-b54b-f07542ecb296" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527493} {:answer/id #uuid "a64f5e23-38b3-551e-9f55-fba8ae74a486" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\pi ,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527651} {:answer/id #uuid "ab311e64-5391-5036-a765-998bb36b2623" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{\\pi }{4},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527659}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ab311e64-5391-5036-a765-998bb36b2623" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{\\pi }{4},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527659} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9630a8b-9a3c-56a1-882a-b2e2265a7e4a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527880}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1cf055f3-7724-5017-8bd4-b9d8370b23a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-122943" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=4t\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{t}{\\pi }),\\,y=t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,t\\in (0,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=\\pi $ on the curve. Determine the point at which the normal to the curve at $C$ crosses the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=\\pi $ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4\\pi \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{\\pi })=4\\pi (0)=0, \\\\ y & =\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi =-\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to determine the equation of the normal at the point $(0,-\\pi ).$ For this, we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{t}{\\pi })+4,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{t}{\\pi })+4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=\\pi ,$ the slope $m$ of the tangent line is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=\\pi } & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi }{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{\\pi })+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{4\\cdot 0+4}=-\\,\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope ${m}_{n}$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m$:\n\n$$\n{m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{\\,(-\\frac{1}{4})\\,}=4\n$$\n\nTo calculate the equation of the normal, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(0,-\\pi )$ and the slope ${m}_{n}=4.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(-\\pi ) & =4(x-0) \\\\ y & =4x-\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe normal intersects the $x$-axis when $y=0.$ At this point, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x-\\pi  & =0 \\\\ 4x & =\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},0)$ is the $x$-intercept."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256751 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083280 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "12a5ad95-0cc9-53c5-8e1c-dae35d1c4447" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Points Where the Normal is Horizontal or Vertical"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38170285-5909-5a87-b81e-7614b331e561" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\pm \\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254153} {:answer/id #uuid "61b95ad1-c677-5a1d-9693-7fe23f0898f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\pm \\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254545} {:answer/id #uuid "74ee3251-ef46-57f3-8565-b37dbcb4bbe3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\pm 3\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254706} {:answer/id #uuid "9f808726-09d3-5692-a435-5070f152cddc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\pm 2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255075} {:answer/id #uuid "cd387a61-32a4-5106-b17f-5c138d6c2ae1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\pm \\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255494}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd387a61-32a4-5106-b17f-5c138d6c2ae1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\pm \\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255494} :answer-field/id #uuid "7461ea8a-3c02-5867-96b2-21f3b9f03a9b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256136}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9e5d7b42-493e-5694-92f6-e6ea1c2f15fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-122946" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=7(t-1),\\,y=2{t}^{3}-18t,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nDetermine the points at which the normal to the curve is vertical." :question/worked-solution "The normal is vertical if the tangent is horizontal. Therefore, we require ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=7,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=6{t}^{2}-18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n6({t}^{2}-3)=0\\,⟹\\,t=\\pm \\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(-\\sqrt{3}) & =7\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\sqrt{3}) & =7\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed vertical when $t=\\pm \\sqrt{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527944 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083280 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "12a5ad95-0cc9-53c5-8e1c-dae35d1c4447" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Points Where the Normal is Horizontal or Vertical"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2066933a-e4ff-5d1b-ba2e-6721a2c0beee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527379} {:answer/id #uuid "2078c7d8-986e-5ee7-aa63-51282c836e74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527380} {:answer/id #uuid "84da7f6b-0d7b-57aa-b191-586787cecf86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527564} {:answer/id #uuid "926b354b-27ea-5d05-9a3e-0c0abaf06914" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527596} {:answer/id #uuid "e8d7db2e-a3aa-5e45-b181-a6699caf9368" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=-\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527765}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "926b354b-27ea-5d05-9a3e-0c0abaf06914" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527596} :answer-field/id #uuid "2bffa8b9-64ed-5f9b-b634-ff2bf41846ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527816}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5a6c8612-f46a-5c05-98fb-598338ec8310" :question/math-academy-id "q-122948" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=1-{e}^{3t},\\,y=4{t}^{3},\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nDetermine the value of the parameter $t$ at which the normal line to the curve is vertical." :question/worked-solution "The normal line is vertical when the tangent to the curve is horizontal. Therefore, we require ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-3{e}^{3t},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=12{t}^{2}\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n12{t}^{2}=0\\,⟹\\,t=0.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(0)=-3{e}^{3(0)}=-3\\ne 0,\\,\\,\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed vertical when $t=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083547 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083245 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "898e04b8-1b7e-59e7-b03f-3ece54aa093b" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Normal Line to a Parametric Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1129c14f-0c46-52c4-9b9a-b4200ce4b285" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272542} {:answer/id #uuid "5e5faf98-bf15-5a2b-9fdc-09a9a727c6b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273199} {:answer/id #uuid "5f37ca8d-3ea5-5dc1-85e2-3a3270cb4ca0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273206} {:answer/id #uuid "7e745226-ecb3-5bd0-91a4-532e194cf6a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273471} {:answer/id #uuid "818af87a-0a7b-5074-95df-e9381f58f773" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2x+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273496}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1129c14f-0c46-52c4-9b9a-b4200ce4b285" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272542} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb3ec910-ef36-5928-896c-acb859df3e11" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274961}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cbab7bcf-6a8b-5a33-ae85-61d0acf13fd7" :question/math-academy-id "q-15570" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,y=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,,\\,t\\in (0,\\pi ).\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ on the curve. Find the equation of the normal to the curve at $C.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})]=\\operatorname{ln}⁡1=0, \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the normal at the point $(0,1).$ For this, we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{{sin}^{2}⁡t}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$  the slope $m$ of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=\\pi /2} & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope ${m}_{n}$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m$:\n\n$$\n{m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{\\,(-1)\\,}=1\n$$\n\nTo calculate the equation of the normal, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(0,1)$ and the slope ${m}_{n}=1.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-1 & =1\\cdot (x-0) \\\\ y & =x+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288015 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083278 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fc81cba-b997-54bc-9e11-0a4ff130833e" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Points Where the Tangent is Horizontal or Vertical"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02788049-040e-5c76-86e2-58bb6a5bbbef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284808} {:answer/id #uuid "5833d2bf-74d0-5ea2-94bc-f893f8489ff8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285598} {:answer/id #uuid "98173c7f-8ba4-5f06-88fe-3425d308e469" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286180} {:answer/id #uuid "ab587a99-40a5-5596-90ea-b5a5e96c6204" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286350} {:answer/id #uuid "e27e9371-7143-528d-820d-b073e0dc0b31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286851}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "02788049-040e-5c76-86e2-58bb6a5bbbef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284808} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f6ee0e5-3926-5962-8761-13aa6562ef64" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287282}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d6e05e4c-c6f0-521f-b67c-d81eff855827" :question/math-academy-id "q-20853" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=2(t+1),\\,y=4{t}^{2},\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nDetermine the value of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is horizontal." :question/worked-solution "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is horizontal if ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=8t.\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n8t=0\\,⟹\\,t=0.\n$$\n\nNote that ${x}^{′}(0)=2\\ne 0.$ Therefore, we conclude that when $t=0,$ the tangent to the curve is horizontal."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083612 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083280 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "12a5ad95-0cc9-53c5-8e1c-dae35d1c4447" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Points Where the Normal is Horizontal or Vertical"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b0735403-c4cc-5f1c-b3f3-f20e814b4e0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439583} {:answer/id #uuid "18c5b8fc-dc2a-5f66-aba9-2fb2139b0e8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439584}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "18c5b8fc-dc2a-5f66-aba9-2fb2139b0e8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439584} :answer-field/id #uuid "45f84d85-7a93-543f-94bb-a475c80511cd" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440149} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7325a515-140f-5d60-bed7-46dc88237008" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439585} {:answer/id #uuid "76c0b0ff-3576-50aa-ae9a-450fb61d13ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439586}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "76c0b0ff-3576-50aa-ae9a-450fb61d13ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439586} :answer-field/id #uuid "23d3ed3a-04d8-5745-91e6-80f3e4c230a7" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440150} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a631b20-d142-5c85-8a98-edc055f0d25f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439587} {:answer/id #uuid "2b5cc863-692b-5dad-a794-3874acf00aa2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439588}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8a631b20-d142-5c85-8a98-edc055f0d25f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439587} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc1b0da1-c6f8-5d48-bed9-a7f7760363df" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440151}] :question/id #uuid "57cb918d-01c6-5793-92dd-c253fd54156c" :question/math-academy-id "q-21080" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n$x = 4\\cos 2t + 5, y = 5\\sin 2t + 4, 0 \\le t < π$.\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the normal to the curve is horizontal.\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked.\n\n- **I.** $t=0$\n- **II.** $t=\\frac{\\pi}{2}$\n- **III.** $t=\\pi$" :question/worked-solution "A vertical tangent (and horizontal normal) requires x′(t)=0 and y′(t)≠0. Here $x′(t)=- 8 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}$ and $y′(t)=10 \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}$. Within the given half-open interval the solutions are $\\left\\{0, \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right\\}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527941 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083244 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "145eff6a-ad61-5001-ab14-cf77e93a7958" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Tangent Line to a Parametric Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4788896b-351b-534b-9161-6b8198197280" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527446}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4788896b-351b-534b-9161-6b8198197280" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527446} :answer-field/id #uuid "30e14e4c-2726-5102-a2db-8f810931d2d9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527818}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "52b01e00-7882-5db4-abc3-b8cfaaac3472" :question/math-academy-id "q-350328" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=2{t}^{5}-4{t}^{3},\\,y={t}^{2},\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nFind the Cartesian equation of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=1.$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of the tangent line is $y=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point by substituting $t=1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2({1}^{5})-4({1}^{3})=2-4=-2 \\\\ y & ={1}^{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we're looking to find the equation of the tangent at the point $(-2,1).$ \n\nTo find the equation of the tangent line, we need to find $\\frac{dy}{dx}.$ To compute it, we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=10{t}^{4}-12{t}^{2},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=2t\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2t}{10{t}^{4}-12{t}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=1,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t\\,=\\,1} & =\\frac{2(1)}{10({1}^{4})-12({1}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{10-12} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(-2)} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the Cartesian equation of the tangent line, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(-2,1)$ and the slope $m=-1.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-1 & =-1(x-(-2)) \\\\ y-1 & =-(x+2) \\\\ y & =-x-2+1 \\\\ y & =-x-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the equation of the tangent line  in slope-intercept form is $y=-x-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527993 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083244 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "145eff6a-ad61-5001-ab14-cf77e93a7958" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Tangent Line to a Parametric Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18c82db0-9172-53d3-850a-85947c46a5d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527358}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "18c82db0-9172-53d3-850a-85947c46a5d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527358} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc7e3261-08d2-5e99-8825-f84a4ffcfbd5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527907} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e67e0c17-5d27-54f5-a8e6-a2baf1d7260c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527761}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e67e0c17-5d27-54f5-a8e6-a2baf1d7260c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527761} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff55a139-4c0c-56e8-a3f1-ea5a3e41bb14" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527909}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "cdc93f1c-a598-5c34-91ec-6e0adc895b1e" :question/math-academy-id "q-350343" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={t}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}t+\\frac{3}{2},\\,y={t}^{3}+7{t}^{2}+8t,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=-1$ on the curve. Determine the coordinates of the point at which the tangent to the curve at $C$ crosses the $y$-axis. Do not round the answers.\n\nThe tangent to the curve at $C$ crosses the $y$-axis at $(${{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=-1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =(-1)^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}(-1)+\\frac{3}{2}=1-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{3}{2}=2 \\\\ y & =(-1)^{3}+7(-1)^{2}+8(-1)=-1+7-8=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we're looking to find the equation of the tangent line to the curve at the point $(2,-2).$ \n\nTo find the equation of the tangent line, we need to find $\\frac{dy}{dx}.$ To compute it, we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=2t+\\frac{1}{2},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=3{t}^{2}+14t+8\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{t}^{2}+14t+8}{2t+\\frac{1}{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=-1,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t\\,=\\,-1} & =\\frac{3(-1)^{2}+14(-1)+8}{2(-1)+\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{(-\\frac{3}{2})} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the equation of the tangent line, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(2,-2)$ and the slope $m=2.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(-2) & =2(x-2) \\\\ y+2 & =2x-4 \\\\ y & =2x-4-2 \\\\ y & =2x-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe tangent crosses the $y$-axis when $x=0.$ At this point, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2(0)-6 \\\\ y & =0-6 \\\\ y & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the tangent to the curve at $C$ crosses the $y$-axis at  $(0,\\,-6).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319374 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083278 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fc81cba-b997-54bc-9e11-0a4ff130833e" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Points Where the Tangent is Horizontal or Vertical"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12952b5e-889f-54cc-a122-5d50215660f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317625}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "12952b5e-889f-54cc-a122-5d50215660f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317625} :answer-field/id #uuid "162eb28c-9915-5e71-854b-15570d6140d4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318471} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dc186f89-e80b-5eb4-a0f9-50d2a3804e90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318301}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc186f89-e80b-5eb4-a0f9-50d2a3804e90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318301} :answer-field/id #uuid "45248884-e4ee-5dd3-b87d-9707883c9d1c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318573}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8ca39289-4112-5812-a40f-16d38e719123" :question/math-academy-id "q-350359" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={t}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}t+\\frac{1}{2},\\,y={t}^{3}+7{t}^{2}+8t,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is horizontal. Do not round the answers.\n\nIn **ascending order**, the tangent to the curve is horizontal at $t=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $t=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is horizontal if ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=2t+\\frac{1}{2},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=3{t}^{2}+14t+8\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{t}^{2}+14t+8 & =0 \\\\ (3t+2)(t+4) & =0 \\\\ t & =-4,-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(-4) & =2(-4)+\\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{15}{2}\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(-\\frac{2}{3}) & =2(-\\frac{2}{3})+\\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{5}{6}\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the tangent to the curve is indeed horizontal at $t=-4$ and $t=-\\frac{2}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528000 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083278 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fc81cba-b997-54bc-9e11-0a4ff130833e" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Points Where the Tangent is Horizontal or Vertical"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a884323-b2b1-59cb-b00e-c84278eaab0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527511}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6a884323-b2b1-59cb-b00e-c84278eaab0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527511} :answer-field/id #uuid "fef39881-b36f-5f93-b608-86d132bcd59d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527908}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d888db88-904a-5f76-93b9-4129d0fd45c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-350373" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx={t}^{3}-6{t}^{2}+12t,\\,y=4{t}^{2}-3t,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nDetermine the value of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is vertical. Do not round the answer.\n\nThe tangent to the curve is vertical at $t=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is vertical if ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${y}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=3{t}^{2}-12t+12,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=8t-3\n$$\n\nSolving ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{t}^{2}-12t+12 & =0 \\\\ 3({t}^{2}-4t+4) & =0 \\\\ 3{(t-2)}^{2} & =0\\,⟹\\,t=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we note that:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(2) & =8(2)-3\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the tangent to the curve is vertical at $t=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527934 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083278 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fc81cba-b997-54bc-9e11-0a4ff130833e" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Points Where the Tangent is Horizontal or Vertical"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2af6dba2-cefe-540b-8ae2-f822fecac4db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527403}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2af6dba2-cefe-540b-8ae2-f822fecac4db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527403} :answer-field/id #uuid "377436fb-63b0-5a7f-9bbf-0d04a4c1e2f0" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527824} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8aaf9d2a-7bf4-5fae-949b-2222f508d67a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527582}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8aaf9d2a-7bf4-5fae-949b-2222f508d67a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527582} :answer-field/id #uuid "703919d5-cced-54a9-9d55-c7c534870732" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527855}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "43c9679e-fdc0-5c6d-a6ac-430a80de179e" :question/math-academy-id "q-350382" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t),\\,y={e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t),\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is vertical. Do not round the answers.\n\nIn **ascending order**, the tangent to the curve is vertical at $t=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $t=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is vertical if ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${y}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)={e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)),\\,{y}^{′}(t)={e}^{t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))\n$$\n\nSolving ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) & =0 \\\\ 0 & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)} & =1 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡(t)=1 \\\\ t & =\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{5\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & ={e}^{\\frac{\\pi }{4}}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})]=\\sqrt{2}{e}^{\\frac{\\pi }{4}}\\ne 0, \\\\ {y}^{′}(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}) & ={e}^{\\frac{5\\pi }{4}}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})]=-\\sqrt{2}{e}^{\\frac{5\\pi }{4}}\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the tangent to the curve is vertical at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and $t=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527972 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083245 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "898e04b8-1b7e-59e7-b03f-3ece54aa093b" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Normal Line to a Parametric Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "716c77ef-6905-55d3-a9a4-8d54e5510e60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527520}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "716c77ef-6905-55d3-a9a4-8d54e5510e60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527520} :answer-field/id #uuid "65d17d6d-2bde-5720-8a1c-bab17264def6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527850}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "947f538f-26d4-542e-9645-e3e57363dc38" :question/math-academy-id "q-350384" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=2{t}^{5}-4{t}^{3},\\,y={t}^{2},\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=1$ on the curve. Find the Cartesian equation of the normal to the curve at $C.$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of the normal line at $C$ is $y=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point by substituting $t=1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2({1}^{5})-4({1}^{3})=2-4=-2 \\\\ y & ={1}^{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we're looking to find the equation of the normal at the point $(-2,1).$ For this, we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=10{t}^{4}-12{t}^{2},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=2t\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2t}{10{t}^{4}-12{t}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=1,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t\\,=\\,1} & =\\frac{2(1)}{10({1}^{4})-12({1}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{10-12} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(-2)} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope ${m}_{n}$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m:$\n\n$$\n{m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{\\,(-1)\\,}=1\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the Cartesian equation of the normal, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(-2,1)$ and the slope ${m}_{n}=1.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-1 & =1(x-(-2)) \\\\ y-1 & =x+2 \\\\ y & =x+2+1 \\\\ y & =x+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the equation of the normal line  in slope-intercept form is $y=x+3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527965 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083245 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "898e04b8-1b7e-59e7-b03f-3ece54aa093b" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Normal Line to a Parametric Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d9012e4-4080-5eed-b0f1-ea73de461ec2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527374}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d9012e4-4080-5eed-b0f1-ea73de461ec2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527374} :answer-field/id #uuid "23614fcf-fbe4-5be9-ae9e-34482a74c5f3" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527812} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c25e94b-079d-51a9-bf98-4849a2d4378b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527369}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1c25e94b-079d-51a9-bf98-4849a2d4378b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527369} :answer-field/id #uuid "298c6150-0d93-5585-9a25-200dda26c396" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527815}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8516a769-888c-5185-902e-1a5250ccaa04" :question/math-academy-id "q-350393" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,0<t<2\\pi .\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ on the curve. Determine the coordinates of the point at which the normal to the curve at $C$ crosses the $y$-axis. Do not round the answers.\n\nThe normal to the curve at $C$ crosses the $y$-axis at $(${{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=3\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}=\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we're looking to determine the equation of the normal at the point $(\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$ For this, we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cot}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t\\,=\\,\\frac{\\pi }{6}} & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope ${m}_{n}$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m:$\n\n$$\n{m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})}=\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3}}=\\sqrt{3}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the equation of the normal, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{1}{2})$ and the slope ${m}_{n}=\\sqrt{3}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-\\frac{1}{2} & =\\sqrt{3}(x-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\ y-\\frac{1}{2} & =\\sqrt{3}x-\\frac{3(3)}{2} \\\\ y & =\\sqrt{3}x-\\frac{9}{2}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ y & =\\sqrt{3}x-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe normal crosses the $y$-axis when $x=0.$ At this point, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the normal to the curve at $C$ crosses the $y$-axis at  $(0,\\,-4).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527971 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083244 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "145eff6a-ad61-5001-ab14-cf77e93a7958" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Tangent Line to a Parametric Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9faef563-301f-5002-ae66-16138801d477" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-4=\\frac{7}{3}(x-10)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527636} {:answer/id #uuid "c7de3c3f-b559-579e-a3ef-c39be1c56cc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-2=7(x-10)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527708} {:answer/id #uuid "e48643fb-0c56-523a-9ebc-a1564cea5906" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-4=\\frac{14}{3}(x-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527757} {:answer/id #uuid "f25addc9-667b-53c2-8974-4909d91dac02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+2=\\frac{7}{6}(x-10)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527779} {:answer/id #uuid "f4b72991-09c1-5e53-ab67-51fa799187db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+4=\\frac{3}{7}(x-10)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527784}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9faef563-301f-5002-ae66-16138801d477" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-4=\\frac{7}{3}(x-10)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527636} :answer-field/id #uuid "84a0580e-1530-5142-8694-ea6e03c23e1e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527863}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9473c03d-7209-5d2d-815a-9982b2c23f32" :question/math-academy-id "q-75267" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=2{t}^{2}+8t,\\,y=4{t}^{7},\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nCalculate the equation of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point by substituting $t=1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2(1)^{2}+8(1)=2+8=10, \\\\ y & =4(1)^{7}=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the tangent at the point $(10,4).$ \n\nTo find the equation of the tangent line, we need to find $\\frac{dy}{dx}.$ To compute it, we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=4t+8,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=28{t}^{6}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{28{t}^{6}}{4t+8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=1,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=1} & =\\frac{28\\cdot (1)^{6}}{4\\cdot (1)+8} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the equation of the tangent line, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(10,4)$ and the slope $m=\\frac{7}{3}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-4 & =\\frac{7}{3}(x-10).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527979 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083280 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "12a5ad95-0cc9-53c5-8e1c-dae35d1c4447" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Points Where the Normal is Horizontal or Vertical"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c0acc55-8e3c-53a4-99b4-5214b54f65a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527368} {:answer/id #uuid "5caf1410-dca6-5dc8-b2ee-176e9278f3aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527483} {:answer/id #uuid "88e0bd31-ef4f-539a-8935-8ba431e9c39a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527575} {:answer/id #uuid "eec6ec5f-a4e6-54e8-99cf-c1af592a1edb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527771} {:answer/id #uuid "f7f10241-5a93-5b9a-b65e-e6638b2d8c44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527789}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eec6ec5f-a4e6-54e8-99cf-c1af592a1edb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527771} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba41f178-68e0-563a-b1ab-e2a541af6814" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527885}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ab667ef1-091e-5d1a-aa96-b7c759cdc55d" :question/math-academy-id "q-75268" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={e}^{-t},\\,y=5{t}^{2}+1,\\,0\\le t\\le 2.\n$$\n\nDetermine the value of the parameter $t$ at which the normal line to the curve is vertical." :question/worked-solution "The normal line is vertical when the tangent to the curve is horizontal. Therefore, we require ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-{e}^{-t},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=10t\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n10t=0\\,⟹\\,t=0.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(0)=-{e}^{-0}=-1\\ne 0,\\,\\,\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed vertical when $t=0.$"}]}
